<p><strong>Quick answer:</strong> in Metro Vancouver a strata property needs a landscaping visit every week from March to October and every two weeks from November to February, with four extra service blocks layered on top: a spring cleanup, two or three hedge trims, a fall leaf program and a winter snow-and-ice plan. A contract that only says "weekly mowing" leaves half of that undone.</p>

<h2>Why the answer is a calendar, not a number</h2>
<p>Lawns in Vancouver grow hardest in April and May, slow almost to a stop under summer watering restrictions, then surge again in September. Garden beds have their own rhythm, hedges another, and leaves and snow arrive on their own schedule. Councils that ask "how many visits a month" get a number that is right for June and wrong for every other month. The useful question is what needs doing in each month, and whether the contract covers it.</p>
<p>The calendar below is what we schedule for the strata and townhouse properties we maintain across Vancouver, Burnaby and Richmond. Every property differs in size and planting, but the sequence is the same.</p>

<h2>January and February: every two weeks, plus winter response</h2>
<ul>
<li>Bi-weekly site visits: litter and debris, drains and catch basins checked, storm damage cleared, hard surfaces blown clean.</li>
<li>Dormant pruning of deciduous shrubs and small trees while the structure is visible.</li>
<li>Snow and ice response as weather demands. Vancouver's bylaw requires sidewalks bordering a property to be cleared of snow and ice by 10 a.m. the morning after a snowfall, and the strata corporation is the property owner for common property. Burnaby and Richmond have similar rules; check the wording for your municipality.</li>
</ul>

<h2>March: the spring cleanup</h2>
<p>One larger visit resets the property before growth starts: beds weeded and cut back, perennials and ornamental grasses cut down, edges re-cut, winter debris removed, lawns raked or power-raked where moss and thatch have built up, and the first mow of the year. This is also when aeration, lime and the first fertilizer application go down on lawns that need them.</p>

<h2>April to June: weekly, and the busiest quarter of the year</h2>
<ul>
<li>Weekly mowing and edging. Grass can grow several centimetres a week in May; skipping a visit shows immediately.</li>
<li>Bed maintenance on rotation: weeding, deadheading, staking, mulching where beds were topped up in spring.</li>
<li>First hedge trim in late May or June, after the spring flush. Check hedges for active bird nests first; nesting season runs roughly March to August and active nests are protected under the BC Wildlife Act.</li>
<li>Irrigation start-up and inspection before the May 1 watering restrictions begin.</li>
</ul>

<h2>July and August: weekly, lighter mowing, more bed work</h2>
<p>Metro Vancouver's Stage 1 watering restrictions run from May 1 to October 15 and limit lawn watering to set mornings, so most lawns slow down and some go dormant. Mowing continues weekly but the crew's time shifts to beds, weeding, watering of new plantings by hand where allowed, and the mid-summer hedge trim. Brown patches in August are usually dormancy, not death; they recover in September.</p>

<h2>September and October: weekly, then the fall program</h2>
<ul>
<li>Growth returns with the rain. Weekly mowing continues into October.</li>
<li>Fall aeration and overseeding, the single most effective treatment for thin, mossy coastal lawns, followed by fall fertilizer and lime.</li>
<li>Final hedge trim for species that tolerate it, so hedges hold their shape through winter.</li>
<li>Leaf program begins in mid-October: for heavily treed sites this is two or three dedicated passes, not one visit, with drains and catch basins cleared each time.</li>
</ul>

<h2>November and December: the fall cleanup, then bi-weekly</h2>
<p>The main fall cleanup lands once most leaves are down, usually November: beds put to bed, spent annuals removed, perennials cut back, the final mow, and every surface drain cleared before the heavy rain. After that, visits drop to every two weeks and the winter plan takes over. Salt and de-icer are applied ahead of forecast freezes, not just after snow, because most winter slip hazards in Vancouver are ice after a thaw and refreeze.</p>

<h2>What a strata landscaping contract should actually specify</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Visit frequency by season</strong>, not a flat monthly number.</li>
<li><strong>Named seasonal services</strong> with a month attached: spring cleanup, hedge trims, fall leaf program, fall cleanup, aeration and overseeding.</li>
<li><strong>Winter response terms</strong>: what gets cleared first, salting triggers, and how each visit is documented.</li>
<li><strong>Proof of service</strong>: photos or a report after each visit, so the council does not have to inspect.</li>
<li><strong>Who owns the extras</strong>: storm damage, plant replacement, irrigation repairs and green-waste disposal are the usual disputes.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="faq-list">
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">Is weekly service really necessary in winter?</h3><p class="faq-answer">No. From November to February most Vancouver strata properties are well served every two weeks, provided the contract includes separate snow and ice response and someone is checking drains after storms.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">How many hedge trims a year does a strata need?</h3><p class="faq-answer">Two or three. Late spring after the first flush, mid-summer, and optionally early fall. Fast growers like laurel may need the third; cedar usually does not.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">When should we aerate and overseed?</h3><p class="faq-answer">Early fall, September to mid-October, is the strongest window on the coast. Spring aeration also works, but fall overseeding faces less weed competition and the rain does the watering.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">Why does our lawn look brown every August?</h3><p class="faq-answer">Summer dormancy under watering restrictions. Cool-season grasses shut down in heat and drought and green up again in September. It is not a sign the contractor is doing something wrong, unless it is also full of weeds.</p></div>
<div class="faq-item"><h3 class="faq-question">How do we know the visits are happening?</h3><p class="faq-answer">Ask for photo-verified visits. Every Mowology visit ends with a photo report to the council or property manager, so nobody has to drive over to check.</p></div>
</div>

